      <body>This capability allows representing relationships between objects to build up various kinds of structures. The structures are defined between part views or between part views and part occurrences.&lt;br&gt; Among the structures supported by this capability is a hierarchical assembly structure to represent the relationships between an assembly and its constituents. The Figure &lt;caption&gt;Simplified overview of the AP242 product data management Domain model&lt;/caption&gt; provides an overview of the capability. &lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;ProductDataManagement_domainModel_Overview.png&lt;/name&gt;Simplified overview of the AP242 product data management Domain model&lt;/figure&gt; For representing hierarchical assembly structures two concepts are supported by this capability: the part view based assembly structure concept and the part occurrences based assembly structure concept.&lt;br&gt; The part occurrences based assembly structure concept allows building up a hierarchical assembly structure by an AssemblyDefinition representing the assembly and one or more Occurrence objects representing its constituents.&lt;br&gt; The part view based assembly structure concept allows building up a hierarchical assembly structure by an AssemblyDefinition object representing the assembly and one or more Part-View objects representing its constituents.&lt;br&gt; In the part view based assembly structure concept a specific part occurrence can be identified by a single AssemblyViewRelationship object, i.e. part occurrences can exists only in the context of an assembly structure, whereas in the part occurrences based assembly structure concept, part occurrences can exists independent of an assembly that uses the product occurrences as constituents.&lt;br&gt; Table below expresses the mapping from the part view based assembly structure concept to the part occurrences based assembly structure concept. &lt;table&gt;&lt;name&gt;table_1.png&lt;/name&gt;Mapping from the part view based assembly structure concept to the part occurrences based assembly structure concept&lt;/table&gt; Table below expresses the mapping from the part occurrences based assembly structure concept to the simple assembly structure concept.&lt;table&gt;&lt;name&gt;table_2.png&lt;/name&gt;Mapping from the part occurrences based assembly structure concept to the part view based assembly structure concept&lt;/table&gt; In both concepts the substitution of single assembly relationships is supported. In addition, this capability allows for specifying geometrical relationships and replacement relation-ships between part views. Another structure supported by this Capability is a grouping mechanism that allows representing the grouping of collection members, represented by PartOccurrence objects, to their collection, represented by a CollectionDefinition object.&lt;br&gt; Geometrical relationships can be specified by relating the geometry models of the assembly and its constituents. There is also simplified approach, where only the cartesian transformation is given. In the first case, the AssemblyOccurrenceRelationship references a GeometricRepresentationRelationship which relates the GeometricModels. In the latter case, a CartesianTransformation giving the rotation matrix, scale, and translation vector is specified. The Figure &lt;caption&gt;Geometric relationship by relating geometric models&lt;/caption&gt; and Figure &lt;caption&gt;Geometric relationship by specifying a cartesian transformation&lt;/caption&gt; provide an illustration of the two geometric relationships, by relating geometric models and by specifying a cartesian transformation. &lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;GeometricRelationshipFull.png&lt;/name&gt;Geometric relationship by relating geometric models&lt;/figure&gt; &lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;GeometricRelationshipShort.png&lt;/name&gt;Geometric relationship by specifying a cartesian transformation&lt;/figure&gt; &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt; Product data management is a cornerstone to attach information coming from several other capabilities. The Figure &lt;caption&gt;Illustration of the interactions between some basic concepts on product structure of the Domain model (some links are not represented)&lt;/caption&gt; provides an illustration of such relationship. &lt;br&gt; &lt;figure&gt;&lt;name&gt;domainModel_PDM_concepts.png&lt;/name&gt;Illustration of the interactions between some basic concepts on product structure of the Domain model (some links are not represented)&lt;/figure&gt;</body>
    </ownedComment>
    <name>ProductDataManagement</name>
    <packagedElement xmi:id="_18_4_1_271a0567_1523457925126_639597_249297" xmi:uuid="00d03ad0-1d6b-4e5b-8f9c-908ea4638825" xmi:type="uml:Association">
